WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.519
Welcome back. Working with a handful of tools is easy, but something breaks when your catalog

2
00:00:05.519 --> 00:00:11.439
grows. The model gets quietly worse at choosing among the different options, and all those

3
00:00:11.439 --> 00:00:17.280
definitions start eating your context window. In this video, we'll work through the pair that fixes

4
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:23.200
both problems, the tool search tool, and a flag called defer loading. Let's get into it.

5
00:00:24.159 --> 00:00:30.639
Scoping tools per agent, which we covered earlier, solves this at one scale. What it can't solve is

6
00:00:30.639 --> 00:00:36.560
the catalog itself. Once you're aggregating several servers, the full list runs past roughly

7
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:43.119
30 to 50 tools, and no amount of per agent trimming shrinks a catalog that size. The other

8
00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:49.840
cost is context, and here the numbers get concrete. That kind of setup can burn something like 55,000

9
00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:55.599
tokens on tool definitions alone, before the model does a single useful thing, and you pay

10
00:00:55.599 --> 00:01:02.159
it again on every turn. Here's the fix in one line. Tool search loads only the few tools a given

11
00:01:02.159 --> 00:01:07.919
request actually needs, and in practice, that trims those definition tokens by over 85%.

12
00:01:09.519 --> 00:01:14.879
So how does tool search work? You add a tool search tool to your tools list, and that tool

13
00:01:14.879 --> 00:01:20.639
becomes the model's way of finding others. You still send every tool definition in your request.

14
00:01:20.639 --> 00:01:26.720
The difference is that the tools you mark as deferred stay out of the model's active context.

15
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.199
They're available to be found, but they're not loaded up front. When the model needs something

16
00:01:31.199 --> 00:01:37.680
it doesn't currently have, it runs a search. Sometimes it writes regex patterns, capped at 200

17
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:45.199
characters. Sometimes it uses bm25, with plain natural language queries up to 500. Matching runs

18
00:01:45.199 --> 00:01:51.919
across tool names, descriptions, and argument fields, so keyword-rich descriptions genuinely help.

19
00:01:52.559 --> 00:01:57.680
Matches come back as lightweight references, the API expands into full definitions on demand.

20
00:01:58.879 --> 00:02:04.720
The mechanism that controls all this is a single flag called defer loading. When you set defer

21
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:10.399
loading to true on a tool, you're saying keep this one out of my active context until the model

22
00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:16.240
actually searches for it. That's what shrinks the footprint. Two details trip people up, so let me be

23
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:22.720
precise. First, deferring a tool doesn't mean you leave it out of the request. You still include its

24
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:29.440
full definition every time, because the search needs it there to find it and expand it. Defer

25
00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:35.600
loading controls what enters the context window, not what you send. Second, you can't defer

26
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:41.039
everything. At least one tool has to stay non-deferred, and normally that's the search tool

27
00:02:41.039 --> 00:02:47.440
itself, since the model needs something loaded to start from. Now let's connect this straight back

28
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:52.960
to accuracy, because that's the real payoff. The whole point of deferring tools is to keep the

29
00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:58.639
model's active tool set small and focused at any given moment. Instead of staring at hundreds of

30
00:02:58.639 --> 00:03:04.880
options, the model typically works with just the three to five relevant tools that a search surfaced

31
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.800
for the current request. It's choosing from a short, sharp list rather than a sprawling crowd.

32
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:17.039
And here's the striking result. Because the active set stays small, selection accuracy stays high,

33
00:03:17.039 --> 00:03:21.759
even when your total catalog runs into the thousands of tools. You get the reach of an

34
00:03:21.759 --> 00:03:28.479
enormous library with the clarity of a tiny menu. That combination is exactly what lets these systems

35
00:03:28.479 --> 00:03:34.800
scale without falling off the accuracy cliff we talked about. So when should you actually reach

36
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.479
for tool search? It earns its keep once your tool surface passes a few thresholds. A good rule of

37
00:03:40.479 --> 00:03:46.559
thumb is 10 or more tools available. Another signal is when your tool definitions start running past

38
00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:52.720
10,000 tokens of context, and it's almost a given when you aggregate several servers together,

39
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.559
and suddenly you're sitting on hundreds of tools. In any of those cases, tool search pays off.

40
00:03:58.559 --> 00:04:03.839
But be honest about the flip side. If you have fewer than 10 tools, or if every tool you've

41
00:04:03.839 --> 00:04:09.279
defined gets used on basically every turn, then standard tool calling is simpler, and there's no

42
00:04:09.279 --> 00:04:14.160
reason to add the machinery. Match the tool to the scale of the problem, not the other way around.

43
00:04:15.119 --> 00:04:21.440
Accuracy and context cost are separate problems, and a big tool catalog creates both of them.

44
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:27.600
The model can choose badly among options that all look plausible. Separately, every definition

45
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.679
sits in the window, costing tokens before any work starts. Tool search handles both by keeping

46
00:04:33.679 --> 00:04:40.799
the active set small, but only the context cost is really about size. Accuracy is about how much

47
00:04:40.799 --> 00:04:46.399
the model has to weigh at once. And that's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

